    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p66-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(\\cos t,\\sin t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(I)",
          "worked": "The identity $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ gives the unit circle. At $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the points are $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$. Both coordinates are nonnegative throughout the interval, so the arc is (I).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p66-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$(\\sin t,-\\cos t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(IV)",
          "worked": "The identity $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ gives the unit circle. At $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the points are $(0,-1)$ and $(1,0)$. Since $x\\geq0$ and $y\\leq0$ throughout the interval, the arc is (IV).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p66-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(-\\cos t,\\sin t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(II)",
          "worked": "The identity $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ gives the unit circle. At $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the points are $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1)$. Since $x\\leq0$ and $y\\geq0$ throughout the interval, the arc is (II).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
